From ddf6aebd6dabd2e79abd74d348e9f32338d0520d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neivin Detlefsen <neivin.detlefsen@gmx.de>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 18:24:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] fix: price generated code per bus transfer like the
 interpreter

Generated blocks charged every CPU cycle at the speed of the code bank,
data transfers included: a WRAM access cost 6 master clocks instead of 8
in FastROM code, $4000-$41FF 6 instead of 12, and an internal cycle 8
instead of 6 in slow code. The interpreter prices each bus transfer at
the speed of its address and the remaining cycles at 6, so the same
instructions took different time depending on the tier that ran them.

The cost model now splits every instruction into fetch, data and
internal cycles (snes_cycles.py, "Bus split"). The block constant holds
the fetches at the code speed and the internal cycles at 6; every data
transfer (operand, indirect pointer, stack, return frame) goes through
the paced accessors, now static inline in cpu_state.h, which add the
speed of the address they touch. Indirect pointers are read once into
temporaries instead of once for the bank and again for the address.
The runtime-only extra cycles (D.l, index page cross, taken branch) are
internal and cost 6. The (dp),Y read page cross and MVN/MVP per byte
are charged as well. Dispatch emitters that model a transfer without
performing it charge it with cpu_bus_charge at the dispatch site.

Two smaller timing gaps go with it. The beam frame driver charges the
interrupt entry (2 internal cycles, the pushes and the vector reads,
8 CPU cycles native), which it did not charge at all. A store to $420B
(MDMAEN) ends the block's charge segment: the frame deadline is checked
right after the DMA, so a frame ends at the first instruction boundary
past it instead of at the next block, which once put two DMAs in one
frame.

tests/cpu_diff now compares cycles and bus-priced master clocks against
interp816 for every opcode test and for loop sequences, in slow bank $00
and FastROM bank $80, and runs in run_c_tests.sh; the old cost model
diverges on 3 237 732 of 3 240 000 checks. beam_frame_driver_test checks
the NMI entry cost (old: 0 master clocks).
